// Deep-dive layer: Ezekiel and Daniel. Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  25: [
    [3, `A watchman, and a scroll that tastes like honey`,
      `Ezekiel is commissioned to eat a scroll “written on the front and on the back” with “lamentations and mourning and woe” (2:10), and finds it “as sweet as honey in my mouth” (3:3). The sweetness is a surprise, since the message is a harsh one: the word of God is sweet even when it announces judgement. Then he sits stunned for seven days among the exiles at Tel-abib (3:15). He is made a watchman: if he warns a wicked person and the person does not turn, the person dies but Ezekiel is saved; if he fails to warn, he is held responsible for the blood (3:16-21). The idea of a limited and personal responsibility for speaking, not for results, shapes the book. The prophet is also made mute except when God opens his mouth (3:26-27).`,
      [[`Revelation 10:8-11`, `John eats a scroll that is sweet and then bitter.`], [`Jeremiah 15:16`, `“Your words were found, and I ate them.”`]],
      [`The message is bitter but tastes sweet. What do you make of that?`, `The watchman is responsible for warning, not for the outcome. How does that shape what you owe others?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of the sweetness?`, `God’s word is nourishing even when it brings bad news.`, `The prophet’s obedience makes the bitter message bearable.`]],
    [5, `Hair cut with a sword`,
      `Ezekiel is told to shave his head and beard with a sword, weigh the hair on scales, burn a third in the city, strike a third with the sword around it, and scatter a third to the wind (5:1-4). A few hairs are tucked in his garment, and some of those are thrown into the fire as well. The sign acts out Jerusalem’s fate, and the explanation is stark: “This is Jerusalem. I have set her in the centre of the nations, with countries all around her. And she has rebelled against my rules… more than the nations” (5:5-6). The text says God will do among them “what I have never done, and the like of which I will never do again” (5:9). Ezekiel is among the most physically dramatic of the prophets: he lies on his side for 390 days (4:4-5) and cooks bread over dung (4:12-15).`,
      [[`Isaiah 7:20`, `God’s razor shaves the head and beard of Judah.`], [`Ezekiel 4:9-15`, `The bread cooked over dung, and Ezekiel’s protest.`]],
      [`Ezekiel’s body becomes a performance. How does it change a message when it is acted out?`, `He protests when told to bake bread over human dung, and God relents (4:14-15). What does that say about the relationship between prophet and God?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe a simple physical sign you might use to communicate a hard message to people who would not listen to words.`]],
    [7, `The end has come`,
      `Ezekiel 7 is a poem of doom in short, hammering lines: “An end! The end has come upon the four corners of the land. Now the end is upon you, and I will send my anger upon you… The time has come; the day is near… Doom has come… The rod has blossomed; pride has budded” (7:2-10). Wealth is useless: “They cast their silver into the streets, and their gold is a thing unclean. Their silver and gold are not able to deliver them in the day of the wrath of the LORD” (7:19). The tone reverses the usual gentleness of prophetic poetry. The chapter ends with an image of silence: “They seek peace, but there is none… they seek a vision from the prophet, but the law perishes from the priest and counsel from the elders” (7:25-26). The text is hard to translate in many places.`,
      [[`Amos 8:2`, `“The end has come upon my people Israel.”`], [`Zephaniah 1:14-18`, `A parallel day of wrath.`]],
      [`The poem’s short lines make the doom feel inevitable. What literary tools does the prophet use here?`, `Silver and gold are thrown away. What would you throw away if nothing could save you?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read a prophetic chapter that is only judgement?`, `As part of a larger movement that ends in hope.`, `As a standalone witness to the severity of exile.`]],
    [9, `The glory leaves the temple`,
      `In a visionary tour of the Jerusalem temple (chapters 8-11), Ezekiel sees abominations: an idol at the entrance, elders offering incense to creatures painted on the walls, women weeping for Tammuz, and men with their backs to the temple bowing to the sun (8:1-18). In chapter 9 six executioners and a man in linen with a writing kit pass through the city, marking on the forehead those who “sigh and groan over all the abominations” (9:4). Those unmarked are killed, beginning at the sanctuary. The glory of the LORD, riding a chariot throne of cherubim, rises from the threshold and departs eastward (9:3; 10:18-19; 11:22-23). The departure is the most devastating image in the book: God leaves his own house.`,
      [[`Revelation 7:3`, `The servants of God sealed on their foreheads.`], [`1 Kings 8:10-11`, `The glory fills the temple at its dedication.`]],
      [`The mark goes to those who grieve over the abominations. What does it say about the role of grief in faithfulness?`, `God leaves the temple in stages. What is the effect of seeing the departure slowly?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of the man with the writing kit?`, `A picture of God’s discrimination between the faithful and the unfaithful.`, `A symbol of how grief for injustice is itself a mark of belonging to God.`]],
    [11, `A new heart of flesh`,
      `At the end of the temple vision, Ezekiel hears that the people left in Jerusalem say of the exiles, “They have gone far from the LORD; to us this land is given for a possession” (11:15). God answers that he has been “a sanctuary for a while in the countries where they have gone” (11:16). He promises a return and a transformation: “I will give them one heart, and a new spirit I will put within them. I will remove the heart of stone from their flesh and give them a heart of flesh, that they may walk in my statutes” (11:19-20). The phrase is repeated in 36:26. Jeremiah 31:33 and Psalm 51:10 use similar language. The chapter ends with the glory leaving the city and standing on the Mount of Olives (11:23).`,
      [[`Ezekiel 36:25-27`, `The fuller promise of a new heart and spirit.`], [`Zechariah 14:4`, `The LORD’s feet stand on the Mount of Olives.`]],
      [`God says he will be a “little sanctuary” among the exiles. What does that do to the idea of a holy place?`, `A heart of stone becomes a heart of flesh. What kind of change does that suggest?`],
      [`rate`, `How receptive are you to a change in your heart you cannot produce yourself?`, `Not very`, `Very`]],
    [12, `Packing for exile in public`,
      `Ezekiel is told to pack his bags in daylight, then dig through the wall of his house in the evening and carry his baggage out on his shoulder in the dark with his face covered, as a sign for “the rebellious house” (12:1-7). The explanation: “The prince who is among them will lift his baggage on his shoulder in the dark and will go out… he shall cover his face, that he may not see the land with his eyes” (12:12). This anticipates Zedekiah’s flight and blinding (2 Kings 25:4-7). The chapter also addresses a proverb in circulation: “The days grow long, and every vision comes to nothing” (12:22), to which God answers, “None of my words will be delayed any longer” (12:28). The prophet’s activities respond to scepticism.`,
      [[`2 Kings 25:4-7`, `Zedekiah’s flight and blinding.`], [`2 Peter 3:3-9`, `A later reply to scoffers about delay.`]],
      [`The people say prophecy has come to nothing. How do communities explain unfulfilled warnings?`, `Ezekiel’s sign acts are performed before the exiles’ eyes. Why would they need to see rather than hear?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the proverb about delay show?`, `A common reaction to prophetic warnings.`, `The exiles’ coping strategy for avoiding the message.`]],
    [13, `Whitewash on a wall`,
      `Ezekiel 13 attacks prophets “who prophesy out of their own hearts” (13:2). “They have misled my people, saying, ‘Peace,’ when there is no peace, and when the people build a wall, these prophets smear it with whitewash” (13:10). The wall will collapse in a storm and the whitewash will not help. The text also condemns women who “sew magic bands upon all wrists and make veils for the heads of persons of every stature, in the hunt for souls” (13:18), practising some form of divination or sorcery for gain. The language echoes Jeremiah 6:14 and 23:16-22. The point is a distinction between a word that seeks to please and a word that tells the truth.`,
      [[`Jeremiah 6:13-15`, `“Peace, peace,” when there is no peace.`], [`Matthew 23:27`, `Jesus: “whitewashed tombs.”`]],
      [`“They smear it with whitewash.” What cosmetic fixes are used in your community to hide structural problems?`, `The chapter names men and women among the false prophets. What does it show about the roles?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe a “wall” in your own life or community that is held up by whitewash, and what honest repair would require.`]],
    [14, `Noah, Daniel and Job`,
      `When elders come to inquire, Ezekiel says that people with idols in their hearts will be answered according to their idolatry (14:1-8). Then comes a famous threefold statement: even if Noah, Daniel and Job were in the land, “they would deliver but their own lives by their righteousness” (14:14, 20). The three are ancient models of righteousness, though “Daniel” here is likely the Ugaritic hero Dan’il, a wise judge in the Aqhat legend, not the exilic Daniel of the book of Daniel (the latter would have been a young man when Ezekiel wrote). The point is that a city’s guilt cannot be offset by a righteous few. The chapter sets out a principle of individual responsibility that chapter 18 develops.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 18:1-4`, `“The soul who sins shall die,” on individual accountability.`], [`Genesis 18:23-32`, `Abraham bargains for Sodom, the opposite principle.`]],
      [`Genesis 18 says a few righteous could save a city; Ezekiel says they would not. How do you handle both?`, `“Daniel” may be an ancient hero, not the book’s Daniel. What does that suggest about how names circulated?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the three names?`, `Noah, Daniel and Job as three ancient examples of exceptional righteousness.`, `A statement against collective merit, suited to the exile’s needs.`]],
    [16, `A foundling grows up and betrays her husband`,
      `Ezekiel 16 is the longest allegory in the book: Jerusalem is a newborn left unwashed in the open field, “cast out… on the day that you were born” (16:4-5). God passes by, sees her, and says, “Live!” (16:6). He grows her, clothes her, and marries her. She uses his gifts to prostitute herself with Egypt, Assyria and Babylon, and even sacrifices her children (16:20-21). The chapter claims Samaria and Sodom are her “sisters,” and says she has been worse than them (16:46-52). It ends with an everlasting covenant: “I will remember my covenant with you in the days of your youth, and I will establish for you an everlasting covenant… that you may remember and be ashamed” (16:60-63). The chapter uses violent and sexualised imagery that many modern readers find troubling.`,
      [[`Hosea 2:2-23`, `Hosea’s use of marriage imagery for Israel.`], [`Isaiah 54:4-8`, `A restoration of the wife.`]],
      [`The chapter portrays God’s love and Israel’s betrayal in marital terms. What are the strengths and dangers of this metaphor?`, `How do you read passages that use the vocabulary of sexual violence in prophecy?`],
      [`choose`, `How should a reader approach the language of Ezekiel 16?`, `As a deliberately shocking allegory that the prophet’s audience would have felt sharply.`, `As a problematic text that requires critical reading, especially its gendered violence.`]],
    [17, `Two eagles and a vine`,
      `Ezekiel 17 is a riddle: a great eagle with long wings (Nebuchadnezzar) takes the top of a cedar (Jehoiachin) to a land of trade and plants a seed (Zedekiah) as a low-spreading vine, which turns toward a second great eagle (Egypt) (17:1-8). The explanation is political: Zedekiah broke his oath to Babylon (17:11-21). The oracle ends with a promise: “I myself will take a sprig from the lofty top of the cedar and plant it… on the mountain height of Israel… and it will bear branches and produce fruit and become a noble cedar” (17:22-23). The image of a tender shoot planted by God is among the messianic images, alongside Isaiah 11:1. “And all the trees of the field shall know that I am the LORD, I bring low the high tree, and make high the low tree” (17:24).`,
      [[`Isaiah 11:1`, `“A shoot from the stump of Jesse.”`], [`2 Kings 24:17-20`, `Zedekiah’s rebellion.`]],
      [`The prophet treats a broken treaty as a sin against God. How does a political oath become a religious one?`, `The riddle ends with a reversal of high and low. What does that say about God’s politics?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the final promise suggest?`, `God himself will restore the Davidic line.`, `A reversal of status that applies to any kingdom.`]],
    [19, `A lament for princes, and a vine plucked up`,
      `Ezekiel 19 is a *qinah*, a funeral lament, for “the princes of Israel” (19:1). It uses two metaphors. The first is of a lioness who raises two cubs: one is caught and taken in chains to Egypt (Jehoahaz), and the second is captured in a pit and carried to Babylon (Jehoiachin or Zedekiah) (19:2-9). The second is of a vine planted by water, fruitful and strong, which is uprooted in fury and thrown down, its strong stem withered and burned (19:10-14). The chapter ends with a line that serves as the book’s epitaph for the monarchy: “This is a lamentation and has become a lamentation” (19:14). The poem shows the prophet using the literary form of the funeral song for the political class.`,
      [[`Genesis 49:9`, `Judah as a lion’s whelp.`], [`2 Samuel 1:19-27`, `David’s lament for Saul and Jonathan.`]],
      [`The prophet writes a lament for his kings. What does it mean to mourn institutions that failed?`, `The lioness and the vine both end in loss. What do you make of the double image?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short lament for a leader or institution you once believed in, using two images.`]],
    [20, `Statutes that were not good`,
      `Ezekiel 20 retells Israel’s history as a history of rebellion, from Egypt to the wilderness. Its most disturbing statement is in 20:25-26: “Moreover, I gave them statutes that were not good and rules by which they could not have life, and I defiled them through their very gifts in their offering up all their firstborn, that I might devastate them.” Interpreters differ on whether this refers to a law requiring the sacrifice of firstborn children (Exodus 22:29, read in a certain way) or to the consequences of Israel’s disobedience, or is an ironic judgement. Jewish and Christian commentators have worked hard to explain the verse. The chapter ends with a promise that Israel will return “on my holy mountain” and “you shall know that I am the LORD” (20:40-44).`,
      [[`Exodus 22:29-30`, `The law of the firstborn.`], [`Romans 1:24-28`, `God “gave them up” to their desires.`]],
      [`How do you read a text that says God gave “not good” laws? What are the options?`, `The chapter is a long history of rebellion. What does it say about how the exiles understood their own past?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Ezekiel 20:25?`, `As a statement that God let them follow harmful practices as a judgement.`, `As a reference to a distorted reading of the firstborn law.`]],
    [21, `The sword of the LORD`,
      `Ezekiel 21 is a sword song: “A sword, a sword is sharpened and also polished, sharpened for slaughter, polished to flash like lightning” (21:9-10). The prophet is told to groan with breaking heart and bitter grief (21:6) to explain the coming news. The king of Babylon stands at a fork in the road and uses divination: “He shakes the arrows, he consults the teraphim, he looks at the liver” (21:21), and decides to attack Jerusalem rather than Rabbah of Ammon. The chapter ends with a message for the “profane wicked prince of Israel” (Zedekiah): “Remove the turban and take off the crown… exalt that which is low, and bring low that which is exalted. A ruin, ruin, ruin I will make it. It shall not be restored until he comes, the one to whom judgement belongs, and I will give it to him” (21:26-27). The last line was read later as a messianic promise.`,
      [[`Genesis 49:10`, `“Until Shiloh comes,” another cryptic promise to Judah.`], [`Matthew 10:34`, `“I have not come to bring peace, but a sword.”`]],
      [`The Babylonian king’s divination points toward Jerusalem. How does the prophet make a pagan ritual serve God’s purpose?`, `“Until he comes to whom judgement belongs.” How does this line anticipate a future king?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the phrase “the one to whom judgement belongs”?`, `As a reference to Nebuchadnezzar.`, `As a veiled messianic promise of a rightful Davidic king.`]],
    [22, `Looking for someone to stand in the breach`,
      `Ezekiel 22 is an indictment of Jerusalem, “the bloody city,” listing its sins: bloodshed, contempt for parents, oppression of the sojourner, orphan and widow, profaning Sabbaths, slander, adultery, bribery and extortion (22:1-12). God will smelt the people like dross in a furnace (22:17-22). The chapter ends with a general survey: priests violate the law, officials are like wolves, prophets whitewash, and the people practise extortion (22:23-29). Then comes a heartbreaking line: “And I sought for a man among them who should build up the wall and stand in the breach before me for the land, that I should not destroy it, but I found none” (22:30). A wall with a breach is a picture of the city under siege, and the “man” is an intercessor like Moses (Psalm 106:23).`,
      [[`Psalm 106:23`, `“Moses… stood in the breach before him.”`], [`Isaiah 59:16`, `“He saw that there was no man.”`]],
      [`God looks for someone to stand in the breach. What is the breach in your community, and who stands there?`, `The chapter lists every layer of society as guilty. What does that do to the possibility of reform?`],
      [`rate`, `How ready are you to stand in a breach that nobody else will?`, `Hesitant`, `Ready`]],
    [23, `Two sisters, Oholah and Oholibah`,
      `Ezekiel 23 is another allegory, even more graphic than chapter 16: two sisters, Oholah (Samaria) and Oholibah (Jerusalem), both belong to God. Oholah lusts after Assyria and is handed over to her lovers. Oholibah, seeing this, is “more corrupt in her lusting” and turns to Babylon and Egypt (23:11-21). The consequences are violent and humiliating, and the chapter ends with a verdict of death by stoning (23:45-49). The imagery of sexual violence is brutal and meant to shock. The names mean “her tent” and “my tent is in her.” The chapter uses the language of prostitution as a metaphor for political alliances. Many readers find it difficult and discuss whether the metaphor risks blaming women for national failure.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 16:1-63`, `The earlier allegory.`], [`Hosea 2:2-13`, `Hosea’s version.`]],
      [`The sisters stand for cities that made foreign alliances. How should a reader approach language that blames women for politics?`, `The chapter uses sexual shame as a rhetorical weapon. What are the ethical risks?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read this passage?`, `As an extended political allegory, to be read critically.`, `As part of the book’s theology of covenant faithfulness.`]],
    [24, `The pot, and a wife’s death`,
      `Ezekiel 24 begins with the exact date of the start of the siege of Jerusalem: “In the ninth year, in the tenth month, on the tenth day” (24:1), 15 January 588 BCE. He tells a parable of a cooking pot placed on a fire with choice pieces of meat, and the scum cannot be removed: “Woe to the bloody city, to the pot whose corrosion is in it” (24:6). Then God tells him, “Behold, I am about to take the delight of your eyes away from you at a stroke; yet you shall not mourn or weep” (24:16). His wife dies that evening, and he obeys: no mourning, and the people ask what it means. The answer: when the temple, “the delight of your eyes,” is destroyed, they too will not mourn openly (24:19-24). The prophet’s grief becomes a sign.`,
      [[`Jeremiah 16:1-9`, `Jeremiah’s own life as a sign.`], [`Lamentations 2:11`, `Public grief at the city’s fall.`]],
      [`Ezekiel is not allowed to mourn. What does it cost to hold back grief as a sign?`, `The chapter links personal and communal loss. How does personal grief help you understand public tragedy?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the death of Ezekiel’s wife?`, `A costly sign that God demanded.`, `A narrative in which the prophet’s life is wholly bound to his message.`]],
    [26, `Tyre, and a ship that sinks`,
      `Ezekiel 26-28 are oracles against Tyre. Chapter 26 says that Tyre exulted over Jerusalem’s fall (“Aha, the gates of the peoples are broken”) and that Nebuchadnezzar will besiege it (26:2, 7-14). The oracle says many nations will come against her “as the sea brings up its waves” and “they will scrape her soil from her and make her a bare rock” (26:3-4). Nebuchadnezzar besieged Tyre for thirteen years (c. 586-573 BCE) without a decisive result, and Ezekiel 29:17-20 admits that his army “had no wages” from it. Alexander the Great later built a causeway to the island city in 332 BCE. The prophecy and its fulfilment have been debated for centuries.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 29:17-20`, `The prophet admits Babylon’s siege did not fully pay off.`], [`Isaiah 23:1-18`, `The earlier oracle on Tyre.`]],
      [`The prophet later admits a prediction was only partly fulfilled. What do you make of that honesty?`, `Why would Tyre’s delight in Jerusalem’s fall bring such an oracle?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read prophecies that are not fulfilled exactly?`, `As conditional or poetic and fulfilled in different ways.`, `As evidence of the human limits of prophets.`]],
    [27, `A lament for a ship of merchants`,
      `Ezekiel 27 is a funeral lament for Tyre pictured as a magnificent merchant ship: “Your borders are in the heart of the seas; your builders made perfect your beauty. They made all your planks of fir trees from Senir; they took a cedar from Lebanon to make a mast for you” (27:4-5). The chapter lists trading partners and goods: silver, iron and tin from Tarshish, slaves and bronze from Javan, horses from Beth-togarmah, wheat from Judah, wine from Damascus (27:12-24). It is one of the most important ancient lists of trade. Then the ship breaks in the sea: “Your riches, your wares, your merchandise… all your crew… sink into the heart of the seas” (27:27). The kings of the earth are appalled, and the merchants “hiss at you” (27:36). Revelation 18 draws on this lament for Babylon.`,
      [[`Revelation 18:11-19`, `Merchants lament Babylon in the language of Ezekiel 27.`], [`Acts 27:9-44`, `A shipwreck in Luke’s narrative.`]],
      [`The list of goods is a picture of a global economy. What parallels do you see with the modern world?`, `The merchants mourn because of lost trade, not because of justice. What does that say about whose grief counts?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short list-lament for a modern system of trade or commerce you depend on, in Ezekiel’s style.`]],
    [28, `The king of Tyre in Eden`,
      `Ezekiel 28 addresses the “prince of Tyre,” who said, “I am a god, I sit in the seat of the gods, in the heart of the seas” (28:2), and announces judgement. A second oracle is a lament over “the king of Tyre,” and its imagery goes well beyond a human ruler: “You were in Eden, the garden of God; every precious stone was your covering… You were an anointed guardian cherub. I placed you; you were on the holy mountain of God… till unrighteousness was found in you” (28:13-15). The passage has been read as a description of Satan’s fall, as in the later Christian tradition, though the immediate target is a human king, and the poem draws on a Phoenician or biblical myth of a primeval figure cast out of Eden. The chapter also has a short oracle against Sidon and a promise that Israel will dwell securely.`,
      [[`Genesis 3:24`, `Cherubim guard the garden of Eden.`], [`Isaiah 14:12-15`, `The taunt song against the king of Babylon.`]],
      [`The prophet uses mythic language about Eden for a king of Tyre. What does that do to the king’s image?`, `Is it faithful to read this as a description of Satan? What do you gain and lose?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the “king of Tyre”?`, `A human ruler described in mythic language of pride and fall.`, `A double reference to a human king and the spiritual power behind him.`]],
    [29, `Pharaoh as a great crocodile`,
      `Ezekiel 29-32 form a group of seven oracles against Egypt. Chapter 29 begins with a date, 7 January 587 BCE: “Behold, I am against you, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon (*tannim*) that lies in the midst of his streams, that says, ‘My Nile is my own; I made it for myself’” (29:3). God will drag him out with hooks in his jaws. Egypt was a “staff of reed” to Israel (29:6), unreliable support, an echo of Isaiah 36:6. The chapter says Egypt will be desolate for forty years and then restored but as “the lowliest of the kingdoms” (29:11-15). A late oracle in 29:17-20 dated to 571 BCE gives Nebuchadnezzar Egypt as pay for Tyre. Pharaoh’s claim to own the Nile is a bold statement of self-sufficiency.`,
      [[`Isaiah 36:6`, `The “broken reed” of Egypt.`], [`Exodus 7:14-25`, `The first plague, on the Nile.`]],
      [`“My Nile is my own; I made it for myself.” What does it mean to claim ownership of a gift?`, `Egypt becomes the lowliest of the kingdoms. How does the prophet imagine the fate of empires?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main target of the oracle?`, `Pharaoh’s pride as a rival to God.`, `Egypt’s unreliability as an ally to Judah.`]],
    [30, `The day of the LORD for Egypt`,
      `Ezekiel 30 continues against Egypt: “Wail, ‘Alas for the day!’ For the day is near, the day of the LORD is near; it will be a day of clouds, a time of doom for the nations” (30:2-3). Cush, Put, Lud and other allies fall with Egypt. God says, “I will break the arms of Pharaoh king of Egypt, and behold, he has not been bound up for healing” (30:21). The Babylonians, “the most ruthless of nations,” will come and the Nile will be dried up (30:11-12). The Babylonian Chronicle records that Nebuchadnezzar fought Egypt in 601 BCE with heavy losses on both sides and invaded Egypt in 568-567 BCE. Chapter 31 compares Pharaoh to a great cedar of Lebanon that is felled, a parable of Assyria’s fall.`,
      [[`Joel 1:15`, `“Alas for the day! For the day of the LORD is near.”`], [`Ezekiel 31:1-18`, `Pharaoh as a cedar in Eden.`]],
      [`The “day of the LORD” belongs to all nations here. What changes when it is not only Israel’s day?`, `A broken arm that is not bound up. How does the image of injury shape the oracle?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read “the day of the LORD” in Ezekiel?`, `As a day of judgement on the nations, including Israel’s enemies.`, `As a day that is both political and cosmic.`]],
    [32, `A lament for Pharaoh, and a descent into Sheol`,
      `Ezekiel 32 contains two laments for Pharaoh. The first pictures him as a lion and a sea monster thrashing in the rivers, caught in God’s net (32:1-16). The second, dated 15 days later, describes the descent to Sheol, where the dead lie: “Whom do you surpass in beauty? Go down and be laid to rest with the uncircumcised” (32:19). Other fallen nations are there in a crowded underworld: Assyria, Elam, Meshech-Tubal, Edom, the princes of the north and the Sidonians (32:22-30). The vision of Sheol as a place where peoples lie “with their weapons of war” has parallels in Isaiah 14 and in Mesopotamian descriptions of the underworld. Ezekiel’s Pharaoh “will be comforted” by seeing the multitude (32:31).`,
      [[`Isaiah 14:9-11`, `Sheol stirs to greet the king of Babylon.`], [`Job 3:13-19`, `Job’s picture of the dead at rest.`]],
      [`The dead are arranged by nation. What picture of the afterlife does this give?`, `The “comfort” of Pharaoh in Sheol is bitter. How do you read the irony?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the underworld scene?`, `As a poetic use of ancient Near Eastern ideas of the afterlife.`, `As a doctrinal statement about the dead.`]],
    [33, `The watchman again, and “Why will you die?”`,
      `Ezekiel 33 reintroduces the watchman image (33:1-9) and states that God takes no pleasure in the death of the wicked: “As I live, declares the Lord GOD, I have no pleasure in the death of the wicked, but that the wicked turn from his way and live; turn back, turn back from your evil ways, for why will you die, O house of Israel?” (33:11). Then news arrives: “The city has been struck down” (33:21), a fugitive tells him in the twelfth year of exile (January 585 BCE). The prophet’s mouth, which had been closed, is opened (33:22). The final section addresses the exiles’ attitude: “They hear your words, but they will not do them… to them you are like one who sings lustful songs with a beautiful voice” (33:31-32). The prophet is entertainment, not a challenge.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 18:23`, `“Have I any pleasure in the death of the wicked?”`], [`2 Peter 3:9`, `God is “not wishing that any should perish.”`]],
      [`God asks, “Why will you die?” How does that question read as a plea?`, `The exiles enjoy Ezekiel’s words and do not act on them. How do you avoid being an audience instead of a respondent?`],
      [`rate`, `How often do you act on what you hear, compared with simply enjoying it?`, `Rarely`, `Often`]],
    [35, `Edom, and the mountains that rejoice`,
      `Ezekiel 35 is an oracle against Mount Seir (Edom), which the prophet accuses of “perpetual enmity” and of taking advantage of Israel’s ruin: “You said, ‘These two nations and these two lands shall be mine, and we will take possession of them’” (35:10). Edom rejoiced at Israel’s desolation and will be made desolate in turn (35:15). The next chapter turns to the mountains of Israel: “O mountains of Israel, hear the word of the LORD… I will cause people to walk on you, even my people Israel. And they shall possess you” (36:8-12). “I will sprinkle clean water on you, and you shall be clean from all your uncleanness… and I will give you a new heart, and a new spirit I will put within you” (36:25-26). The chapter speaks of God acting “not for your sake… but for the sake of my holy name” (36:22).`,
      [[`Obadiah 1:10-14`, `Edom’s sin against its brother.`], [`John 3:5`, `Jesus: “born of water and the Spirit,” echoing Ezekiel 36:25-27.`]],
      [`God says he acts for his own name’s sake. How do you hear that: as comfort or as challenge?`, `Ezekiel 36:26-27 is a promise of heart transplant. How does it compare with a self-improvement project?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does God restore Israel “for the sake of my holy name”?`, `Because his name was profaned by Israel’s exile and must be vindicated.`, `Because God’s character, not Israel’s merit, is the basis of hope.`]],
    [36, `Dry bones`,
      `Ezekiel 37 opens with the prophet carried by the Spirit to a valley full of dry bones. God asks, “Son of man, can these bones live?” Ezekiel answers, “O Lord GOD, you know” (37:3). He prophesies, and the bones come together with a rattling sound, tendons and flesh and skin appear, but there is no breath until he prophesies to the breath (37:7-10). God explains: “These bones are the whole house of Israel. Behold, they say, ‘Our bones are dried up, and our hope is lost; we are indeed cut off’” (37:11). The vision is of national restoration from exile, not individual resurrection, though later readers saw both. The second sign joins two sticks, Judah and Joseph, into one (37:15-23). The whole nation will have one king, “David my servant” (37:24).`,
      [[`Genesis 2:7`, `God breathes into the first human.`], [`John 20:22`, `Jesus breathes on the disciples.`]],
      [`“Can these bones live?” “You know.” What does Ezekiel’s answer teach about faith?`, `The vision begins as national restoration and was later read as resurrection. What is gained and lost in each reading?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe a “valley of dry bones” in your community or life, and what would count as breath.`]],
    [38, `Gog of Magog`,
      `Ezekiel 38-39 describes an invasion of Israel from the far north by Gog of the land of Magog, with allies Persia, Cush, Put, Gomer and Beth-togarmah (38:1-6). Gog comes “in the latter years” to a land “restored from war,” a people “gathered from many nations” who “were brought out from the nations and now dwell securely” (38:8). God destroys the invaders with earthquake, plague, hail and fire (38:19-22; 39:4-6). The identity of Gog is debated: some suggest Gyges, king of Lydia (Gugu in Assyrian texts), or a symbol. Revelation 20:7-9 reuses the names Gog and Magog as symbols of the final rebellion of the nations. The long Jewish and Christian history of reading these chapters as predicting specific modern events should be treated with caution.`,
      [[`Revelation 20:7-10`, `Gog and Magog as symbols of the final revolt.`], [`Ezekiel 39:25-29`, `The restoration that follows.`]],
      [`How do you handle a prophecy that has been applied to many different historical powers?`, `The invasion comes to a people living securely. Why does security draw an attack in the story?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Gog and Magog?`, `As symbols of final opposition to God’s people.`, `As references to specific historical powers, which later readers reapplied.`]],
    [39, `Seven months of burial, and the spirit poured out`,
      `After Gog’s defeat, the chapter describes weapons burned for seven years and the burial of the dead in the Valley of Hamon-gog, which takes seven months (39:9-16). Birds and beasts are summoned to a sacrificial banquet: “You shall eat the flesh of the mighty and drink the blood of the princes of the earth” (39:17-20), an image echoed in Revelation 19:17-18. The exile is explained: “The house of Israel went into captivity for their iniquity” (39:23). Then comes a closing promise: “I will no longer hide my face from them, when I pour out my Spirit upon the house of Israel” (39:29). The scene is a mix of apocalyptic ritual and political hope.`,
      [[`Revelation 19:17-21`, `The great supper of God.`], [`Joel 2:28-29`, `“I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh.”`]],
      [`The cleansing of the land takes seven months. What does that say about the idea of purity in the text?`, `God promises to pour out his Spirit as the last word. How does that reframe the violence?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main purpose of the Gog oracle?`, `To assure the exiles that God will protect the restored community.`, `To provide an apocalyptic vision of a final conflict.`]],
    [40, `A temple measured with a reed`,
      `Ezekiel 40-48, dated to the twenty-fifth year of exile (573 BCE), is a vision of a future temple and land. A man with the appearance of bronze stands at a gate with a flax cord and a measuring reed (40:3), and measures gates, courts, chambers and the sanctuary with great precision. The building is a vast, perfectly symmetrical complex. Ezekiel’s temple differs from Solomon’s temple and from the second temple; no one has built it. Jewish tradition says it will be explained in the messianic age, and Christian readers have taken it as symbolic or as pointing to the church or a literal future temple. The detailed measurements convey a sense of order, holiness and permanence to an exiled people who had lost their own.`,
      [[`Revelation 21:15-17`, `An angel measures the New Jerusalem with a reed.`], [`1 Kings 6:1-38`, `Solomon’s temple.`]],
      [`Why would a prophet give so many measurements for something that did not exist? What does exact detail do for a hopeful vision?`, `The vision is never built. How do you read an unfulfilled blueprint?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read Ezekiel’s temple?`, `As a symbolic vision of God’s restored presence.`, `As a plan for a literal temple in a future age.`]],
    [41, `Cherubim and palm trees`,
      `Ezekiel 41 describes the main hall and the Holy of Holies, and the walls carved with cherubim and palm trees: “Every cherub had two faces: the face of a man toward the palm tree on the one side, and the face of a young lion toward the palm tree on the other side” (41:18-19). The inner room is a perfect square of twenty cubits (41:4), as in 1 Kings 6:20. The text mentions a wooden altar, “the table that is before the LORD” (41:22). The decoration recalls Eden, the garden of God, and Solomon’s temple (1 Kings 6:29). The temple becomes a new garden sanctuary. The chapter concerns holiness in architecture: space is organised in layers of increasing sanctity.`,
      [[`1 Kings 6:29-35`, `Cherubim and palm trees in Solomon’s temple.`], [`Genesis 3:24`, `Cherubim guard Eden.`]],
      [`The temple is full of garden imagery. What does that suggest about its purpose?`, `Space is organised in layers of holiness. How do spaces in your life carry layers of significance?`],
      [`rate`, `How much does physical space shape your sense of the sacred?`, `Little`, `A great deal`]],
    [42, `A wall between the holy and the common`,
      `Ezekiel 42 describes chambers where priests eat the most holy offerings and keep their garments, and ends with a measurement of the whole temple area: a square of 500 reeds on each side, “to make a separation between the holy and the common” (42:20). The idea of a boundary is central to priestly thought (Leviticus 10:10). The priests must change clothes before moving into the outer court (42:14), because the holiness of their garments could harm the people (44:19). The text assumes that holiness is dangerous as well as beneficial. The chapter prepares for chapter 43 and the return of God’s glory. The cold precision of the chapter has a purpose: it sets the stage for presence.`,
      [[`Leviticus 10:10`, `“To distinguish between the holy and the common.”`], [`Ezekiel 44:17-19`, `Priests change garments in the outer court.`]],
      [`Holiness requires boundaries here. What does that say about the way you protect what you value?`, `The chapter is meticulous. Why would detail matter for a vision of presence?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does the temple vision stress separation?`, `To preserve holiness against the failures that led to exile.`, `To show the order of God’s dwelling.`]],
    [43, `The glory returns from the east`,
      `Ezekiel 43 is the climax of the temple vision: “And behold, the glory of the God of Israel was coming from the east. And the sound of his coming was like the sound of many waters, and the earth shone with his glory” (43:2). The glory that left by the east gate (11:23) returns the same way: “As the glory of the LORD entered the temple by the gate facing east, the Spirit lifted me up and brought me into the inner court; and behold, the glory of the LORD filled the temple” (43:4-5). God says, “This is the place of my throne… where I will dwell in the midst of the people of Israel forever” (43:7). The new name of the city appears at the end of the book: “The LORD Is There” (48:35).`,
      [[`Ezekiel 10:18-19`, `The glory leaves toward the east.`], [`Revelation 21:3`, `“The dwelling place of God is with man.”`]],
      [`The glory leaves and returns by the same gate. What does that say about the shape of the book?`, `The vision ends with a name. What name would you give a place that God has returned to?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write four lines about a place in your life that was emptied and where you hope for presence to return.`]],
    [44, `Priests, and the closed gate`,
      `Ezekiel 44 states that the east gate through which the glory entered shall remain shut: “It shall not be opened, and no one shall enter by it, for the LORD, the God of Israel, has entered by it” (44:2). Only “the prince” may sit in it to eat bread before the LORD. The chapter addresses the failure of the Levites who served at idol shrines and are demoted to a lower role (44:10-14), while the Zadokite priests who “kept the charge of my sanctuary when the people of Israel went astray” are given the central role (44:15-16). This distinction between Zadokites and other Levites shaped later priestly structure. Jewish and Christian traditions saw the closed gate as a symbol, and in the church it was applied to Mary’s perpetual virginity.`,
      [[`1 Kings 2:26-27`, `Abiathar removed and Zadok installed.`], [`Numbers 18:1-7`, `The Levites’ duties and limits.`]],
      [`The chapter sets out a hierarchy among priests based on prior faithfulness. How do you think about consequences for earlier failure in leadership?`, `The closed gate has been read in many ways. What does it mean to you?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the Zadokite priority?`, `As a rebuke to those who failed during the apostasy.`, `As a reflection of the political-religious conflict between priestly families.`]],
    [45, `A holy district and fair weights`,
      `Ezekiel 45 sets aside a holy district of 25,000 by 20,000 cubits in the centre of the land for the sanctuary, the priests and the Levites, with a plot for the city and a portion for the prince on either side (45:1-8). The prince’s role is to give his people their allotments: “Enough, O princes of Israel! Put away violence and oppression, and execute justice and righteousness. Cease your evictions of my people” (45:9). The chapter requires honest weights and measures (45:10-12) and lists the prince’s duties in providing offerings at festivals (45:13-25). The vision ties land policy to ethics: the prince must not take away the people’s inheritance (46:18). The “holy portion” gives structure to a society where the temple is the centre and economic justice is part of worship.`,
      [[`Leviticus 19:35-36`, `“You shall do no wrong in judgement, in measures of length or weight.”`], [`1 Kings 21:1-16`, `Naboth’s vineyard, the story that the ban on evictions recalls.`]],
      [`The prince is told to stop evictions. What does that say about how the prophet pictures power?`, `The holy district is at the heart of the land. How would a society look if its worship centre were also its economic guide?`],
      [`choose`, `Why would the vision include land and measures?`, `Because justice in daily life is part of worship.`, `Because the vision aims to restore every aspect of national order.`]],
    [46, `The river from the temple`,
      `Ezekiel 46 gives rules for the prince’s worship and the temple kitchens (46:19-24), and chapter 47 describes a river that flows from beneath the threshold of the temple, getting deeper as it goes: ankle-deep, knee-deep, waist-deep, and then a river that cannot be crossed (47:1-5). It flows east into the Arabah and the Dead Sea, and “the water of the sea will become fresh” (47:8). “Everything will live where the river goes” (47:9). Fruit trees line the banks, with leaves that do not wither and fruit that does not fail, “for their water flows from the sanctuary. Their fruit will be for food, and their leaves for healing” (47:12). Revelation 22:1-2 borrows this image of the river and the tree of life.`,
      [[`Revelation 22:1-2`, `The river of the water of life and the tree.`], [`Genesis 2:10-14`, `The river that flows out of Eden.`]],
      [`The river deepens with each measurement. What does that say about a source of life that grows as it goes?`, `The Dead Sea becomes fresh. What dead place in your life or world would you want healed?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe a “river” in your own community that begins small at its source and grows, with what it would bring to life.`]],
    [48, `The city’s new name`,
      `Ezekiel 48 divides the land among the twelve tribes in parallel strips, from Dan in the far north to Gad in the south, an ideal geography that ignores real topography. The city has twelve gates named for the tribes (48:30-34), recalling Revelation 21:12-13. The book ends with its last line: “And the name of the city from that time on shall be, The LORD Is There” (*Yhwh shammah*) (48:35). It answers the departure of the glory in chapters 8-11. The book moves from a God who leaves because of defilement to a God who comes to dwell in a restored, orderly world. In the Hebrew text, the final word is “there.”`,
      [[`Revelation 21:2-3`, `A new Jerusalem, with God dwelling with humans.`], [`Ezekiel 11:22-23`, `The glory leaves the city.`]],
      [`The book ends with a name that answers the book’s opening vision of absence. How does that ending shape the whole?`, `The tribal map is idealised. What does it say about the function of vision in a time of exile?`],
      [`choose`, `What does “The LORD Is There” mean?`, `A promise of divine presence in a restored Jerusalem.`, `A final theological answer to the exile’s question about God’s absence.`]]
  ],
  26: [
    [1, `Daniel and the vegetables`,
      `Daniel 1 introduces four young men from Judah taken to Babylon, trained for three years in the language and literature of the Chaldeans, and given Babylonian names: Belteshazzar (Daniel), Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. Daniel resolves “not to defile himself with the king’s food, or with the wine that he drank” (1:8) and proposes a test of ten days on vegetables and water. At the end, they look healthier than those who ate the king’s food (1:15). The motive is not clearly dietary law; the food may have been defiled by idol offering, or by the implicit dependence on the king. The book begins with the first deportation in 605 BCE. The language shifts from Hebrew (1:1-2:4a) to Aramaic (2:4b-7:28) and back to Hebrew (8-12).`,
      [[`Genesis 41:14-45`, `Joseph rises in a foreign court, another model.`], [`Esther 4:14`, `A Jew in a Persian court.`]],
      [`Daniel takes a stand over food, a small matter. What does this say about where integrity begins?`, `The book shifts between Hebrew and Aramaic. What does that suggest about its audience and composition?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main lesson of the story?`, `That faithful people can serve in foreign courts without compromising their identity.`, `That small acts of resistance matter, as a model for later persecuted communities.`]],
    [2, `A statue of four metals`,
      `Nebuchadnezzar has a dream he cannot recall and demands that his sages tell him both the dream and its meaning. When they cannot, he orders them killed. Daniel asks for time, prays, and receives a vision, then blesses God: “He changes times and seasons; he removes kings and sets up kings” (2:21). The dream is of a statue with a head of gold, chest of silver, belly of bronze, legs of iron, and feet of iron mixed with clay. A stone “cut out by no human hand” strikes the feet and becomes a great mountain filling the earth (2:34-35). The four kingdoms are traditionally read as Babylon, Media, Persia and Greece (the critical view), or Babylon, Medo-Persia, Greece and Rome (traditional Christian view). The kingdom of God will “never be destroyed” (2:44).`,
      [[`Daniel 7:1-28`, `A parallel vision of four beasts.`], [`Luke 20:17-18`, `Jesus refers to the stone that crushes.`]],
      [`Daniel asks for time and prays before answering. What does that teach about wisdom?`, `Two ways of identifying the fourth kingdom lead to different readings. How do you handle the choice?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the four kingdoms?`, `Babylon, Media, Persia, Greece, the view of most critical scholars.`, `Babylon, Persia, Greece, Rome, the traditional Christian view.`]],
    [4, `A king who becomes an animal`,
      `Daniel 4 is a letter from Nebuchadnezzar, who tells of a dream of a great tree cut down, with its stump bound with iron and bronze and left in the grass. Daniel interprets: the tree is the king, who will be driven from human society and live like an ox for “seven times” until he acknowledges that “the Most High rules the kingdom of men” (4:25). Daniel advises him to “break off your sins by practising righteousness, and your iniquities by showing mercy to the oppressed” (4:27). A year later, as the king boasts about “Babylon the great, which I have built,” the sentence falls (4:30). He is restored after seven periods of time, and praises God. A fragment from Qumran, the *Prayer of Nabonidus*, tells a similar story of the last Babylonian king, Nabonidus, who was struck with illness and lived for seven years in Teima.`,
      [[`Isaiah 14:13-15`, `The king’s boast and fall.`], [`Acts 12:21-23`, `Herod’s pride and sudden death.`]],
      [`The king is told to practise mercy as a way of avoiding judgement. What does that say about repentance?`, `The Qumran text suggests the legend was connected with Nabonidus. How does that affect how you read Daniel 4?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the story show about kingdoms?`, `That human power is a gift, and arrogance is the cause of its loss.`, `That later Jews told stories about foreign kings to encourage faith.`]],
    [5, `The writing on the wall`,
      `At a feast for a thousand nobles, Belshazzar drinks from the gold vessels taken from the Jerusalem temple and praises “the gods of gold and silver, of bronze, iron, wood and stone” (5:4). A hand writes on the plaster: *mene, mene, tekel, parsin* (5:25). The queen mother recalls Daniel, who interprets: “God has numbered your kingdom and brought it to an end… You have been weighed in the balances and found wanting… Your kingdom is divided and given to the Medes and Persians” (5:26-28). That night, Belshazzar is killed. The book calls Belshazzar the son of Nebuchadnezzar, though Babylonian records show he was the son of Nabonidus and co-regent. Daniel’s Darius the Mede (5:31) has no clear historical match either. Scholars debate how to account for these differences.`,
      [[`Isaiah 21:5-9`, `A feast in Babylon and a watchman’s cry of fall.`], [`Jeremiah 51:39-41`, `Babylon’s revelry and death.`]],
      [`Belshazzar uses holy vessels for a drunken feast. What does it say about the casual desecration of what others hold sacred?`, `“Weighed and found wanting.” How would you feel on the scales?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you handle the historical problems in Daniel 5?`, `As a popular tradition with theological emphasis.`, `As history, with different harmonisations of the names and titles.`]],
    [6, `The lions’ den`,
      `Jealous officials trick Darius into signing a decree that for thirty days no one may pray to any god or man except the king. Daniel “got down on his knees three times a day and prayed and gave thanks before his God, as he had done previously” (6:10), with his windows open toward Jerusalem. He is thrown to the lions, and the king, distressed, spends a sleepless night. At dawn Daniel is alive: “My God sent his angel and shut the lions’ mouths” (6:22). His accusers are thrown in, and “the lions overpowered them” (6:24). The story is a companion to Daniel 3, in which Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refuse to bow to the statue and are thrown into the furnace. Hebrews 11:33 refers to those who “stopped the mouths of lions.”`,
      [[`Daniel 3:16-18`, `“But if not,” the three friends’ answer.`], [`Hebrews 11:32-34`, `Heroes of faith who shut lions’ mouths.`]],
      [`Daniel keeps his habit when it becomes illegal. What does that teach about spiritual practice under pressure?`, `The king is the one who suffers a sleepless night. What is the story saying about power and conscience?`],
      [`rate`, `How easily do you keep practices that have become inconvenient or risky?`, `Hard`, `Easy`]],
    [8, `A ram, a goat and a little horn`,
      `Daniel 8 starts the second half of the book, in Hebrew, with a vision of a two-horned ram (Media-Persia) attacked by a one-horned goat from the west (Greece, with its great horn Alexander), whose horn is broken and replaced by four (the successors). Out of one comes “a little horn” that grows, takes away the daily burnt offering, and throws down the sanctuary (8:9-12). The angel Gabriel explains the vision: it concerns “the time of the end” (8:17). “For 2,300 evenings and mornings; then the sanctuary shall be restored to its rightful state” (8:14). Most scholars identify the little horn as Antiochus IV Epiphanes, who profaned the temple in 167 BCE (1 Maccabees 1:20-64). Christian tradition has also read the vision in light of other later powers.`,
      [[`1 Maccabees 1:41-64`, `Antiochus’ desecration of the temple.`], [`Daniel 11:21-35`, `A detailed prophecy of the same period.`]],
      [`The vision identifies kingdoms and then a desecration. How do detailed historical prophecies change how you read the book’s date?`, `“How long?” is the question of the vision. Where does the question arise in your world?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the date of Daniel?`, `Written in the sixth century BCE, as the book claims.`, `Composed during the Maccabean crisis, c. 165 BCE, as most critical scholars hold.`]],
    [10, `A heavenly conflict`,
      `In Daniel 10, after three weeks of mourning, Daniel sees a man in linen with a face like lightning and eyes like flaming torches, and he falls into a deep sleep. The messenger says he was delayed for twenty-one days: “The prince of the kingdom of Persia withstood me twenty-one days, but Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me” (10:13). The chapter pictures angelic beings engaged in a conflict behind the scenes of history, with “princes” of Persia and Greece. Michael is called “your prince” (10:21), the guardian of Israel. Later tradition (Revelation 12:7; Jude 9) pictures Michael as an archangel. The notion that earthly conflicts have heavenly counterparts is an important part of apocalyptic literature.`,
      [[`Ephesians 6:12`, `“Our struggle is not against flesh and blood.”`], [`Revelation 12:7-9`, `Michael and his angels fight.`]],
      [`The angel was delayed by an opposing power for three weeks. What does that say about the relation between prayer and what we see happening?`, `Do you find the idea of angelic princes helpful or distracting? Why?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the “prince of Persia”?`, `A literal angelic being linked to Persia.`, `A symbolic way of speaking about the spiritual dimension of political power.`]],
    [11, `A history before it happens`,
      `Daniel 11 is the most detailed prophecy in the Bible. It traces, in veiled language, the conflict between the Ptolemies (“kings of the south”) and the Seleucids (“kings of the north”) from the Persian period to Antiochus IV: wars, marriages (a daughter given to “make peace,” 11:6), treaties and betrayals. Verses 21-35 describe a contemptible man who seizes the kingdom by intrigue, sets his heart against the holy covenant, and sets up “the abomination that makes desolate” (11:31). The details match the historical record closely up to about 165 BCE and then diverge: the account of Antiochus’ death (11:40-45) does not match what is known, leading most critical scholars to date the book to the Maccabean period. Conservative scholars see supernatural prediction.`,
      [[`1 Maccabees 1:10-64`, `The historical account of Antiochus’ reign.`], [`Mark 13:14`, `Jesus refers to “the abomination of desolation.”`]],
      [`The prophecy is so detailed that it reads like history. How do you decide whether it is prediction or retelling?`, `Jesus quotes the “abomination” phrase. How does that show the text’s reuse?`],
      [`choose`, `What should we make of the accurate and inaccurate parts of the prophecy?`, `A sign of composition in 165 BCE, where the final part is a real prediction that did not happen as stated.`, `A sign of a divine foreknowledge that goes beyond the details, whose final portion refers to a future time.`]]
  ]
};
